5:[["$","$L10","1.10",{"topic":{"id":"1.10","title":"Information Technology Rules, 2018","minutes":11,"summary":"The syllabus label needs precision: the commonly intended 2018 instrument is an amendment to the Intermediary Guidelines Rules, 2011.","sections":[{"title":"Identify the instrument correctly","body":["“Information Technology Rules, 2018” is not a sufficiently precise citation. In this syllabus it is commonly used for the Information Technology (Intermediaries Guidelines) Amendment Rules, 2018. These amended the 2011 Intermediaries Guidelines Rules. They are distinct from the SPDI Rules, 2011 and from the Interception, Monitoring and Decryption Rules, 2009.","The 2018 amendment process generated significant debate and a proposed replacement framework. A draft, a press release, a notified amendment, and a final rule in force are different documents. When citing a rule, record its full title, notification number, date, and operative text. This prevents a proposed obligation from being taught as binding law."]},{"title":"Intermediary due diligence and unlawful content","body":["The 2011 framework required intermediaries to publish rules, privacy policies, and user agreements and to specify categories of prohibited content. The due-diligence conditions were linked to safe harbour under section 79. The Shreya Singhal decision had already affected the interpretation of actual knowledge and the use of the 2011 framework, so reading the bare notification without the judgment is incomplete.","Intermediary obligations depend on the service’s role. Hosting user content, transmitting data, and actively participating in unlawful conduct are not automatically equivalent. The later 2021 Rules replaced the 2011 Intermediaries Guidelines framework, but older events may still require historical analysis. The SPDI Rules are a separate instrument and were not simply replaced by that 2021 replacement clause."]},{"title":"Practical records for a platform response","body":["A defensible platform process records the complaint, the exact URL or identifier, the asserted legal basis, the decision-maker, notice to the affected user where required, and the action taken. A request to preserve records should be distinguished from a request to remove content or disclose subscriber information. Each has different authority and retention implications.","For an exam, explain the naming ambiguity first, then discuss the relevant notified amendment and the later 2021 framework. Avoid inventing a standalone “IT Act, 2018”. The central learning outcome is accurate legal identification, not memorizing an incomplete shorthand from a course outline."]}],"practice":{"title":"Disambiguate a citation","scenario":"An old report says only “under the 2018 IT Rules, the platform had to act”.","steps":["Find the full title and notification date in the source material.","Determine whether the report refers to a draft or a notified amendment.","Check whether the incident predates the 2021 Rules.","Rewrite the citation and explain which version governs the asserted duty."],"debrief":"A precise citation is itself an investigative safeguard. It lets another reviewer reproduce your legal reasoning."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-2018","it-2011","it-2021"],"sources":["rules","shreya","itact"],"labId":"$undefined","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain identify the instrument correctly and apply it to an investigation.","Explain intermediary due diligence and unlawful content and apply it to an investigation.","Explain practical records for a platform response and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.10","prompt":"What should you do with the syllabus phrase “IT Rules, 2018”?","options":["Assume it is a new criminal code.","Identify the full instrument and distinguish amendments from drafts.","Treat every draft as binding law.","Assume it repealed the SPDI Rules."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The commonly intended instrument is the 2018 amendment to the Intermediaries Guidelines Rules, 2011; exact identification matters.","unit":1}}}]]
